//? Title: Fit repeated measures with incomplete follow-up
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Estimate visit means with an unstructured covariance

Participants have a baseline symptom score and two follow-up visits.
In this example, participants with the highest baseline scores skip the
last visit. Each participant contributes the portion of the Gaussian
likelihood corresponding to their observed visits. Restricted maximum
likelihood estimates a separate variance for each visit and a covariance
for each visit pair.

```{cpp, id=incomplete-follow-up, file="figures/mmrm_visit_means.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/mmrm.hpp>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    const std::vector<double> weeks{0,4,8};
    std::vector<double> scores;
    std::vector<std::size_t> participant, visit;
    std::array<double,3> totals{};
    std::array<std::size_t,3> counts{};
    for (int id = 1; id <= 28; ++id) {
        std::array<double,3> outcome{};
        for (int v = 0; v < 3; ++v)
            outcome[v] = 7 - .75*v + (1 + .15*v)*std::sin(.81*id) +
                .27*std::sin(2*id + 3*v) + .32*std::cos(3*id - 2*v);
        for (int v = 0; v < 3; ++v) {
            if (v == 2 && outcome[0] > 7.3)
                continue;
            scores.push_back(outcome[v]);
            participant.push_back(id);
            visit.push_back(v);
            totals[v] += outcome[v];
            ++counts[v];
        }
    }

    const auto fit = stats::fit_mmrm(scores, participant, visit);
    if (fit.status != stats::MMRMStatus::Converged)
        throw std::runtime_error("Expected the repeated-measures likelihood to converge");
    std::vector<double> observed;
    std::println("| Week | Observed scores | Observed mean | Fitted mean | Model SE |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (std::size_t v = 0; v < weeks.size(); ++v) {
        observed.push_back(totals[v] / counts[v]);
        std::println("| {:.0f} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", weeks[v], counts[v], observed[v],
                     fit.coefficients[v], std::sqrt(fit.covariance(v,v)));
    }

    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto plot = graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(weeks, observed, "Observed mean", graphics::RGBA{150,65,35}, 3)
        .line(weeks, fit.coefficients, "Repeated-measures mean", graphics::RGBA{29,78,150}, 3)
        .title("Symptom scores under incomplete follow-up")
        .x_label("Weeks since baseline").y_label("Mean symptom score")
        .size(730,420).save_svg("build/mmrm_visit_means.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Week | Observed scores | Observed mean | Fitted mean | Model SE |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 28 | 7.051 | 7.051 | 0.148 |
| 4 | 28 | 6.332 | 6.332 | 0.163 |
| 8 | 15 | 4.928 | 5.625 | 0.208 |
\image(figures/mmrm_visit_means.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The fitted mean is 0.70 points above the observed mean at week 8.)
\alttext(Two lines overlap at 7.05 at baseline and 6.33 at week 4. At week 8 the observed mean is 4.93 and the fitted mean is 5.63.)

When everyone is observed, the two estimates coincide in this visit-means
model at baseline and week 4. At week 8, the likelihood uses the estimated
within-participant association between earlier and later scores, so the
fitted mean can differ from the mean of the scores still observed.
Inference from this likelihood relies on the Gaussian model and on
missingness being ignorable given the observed data. The reported model
standard errors use the fitted covariance.

[Jennrich and Schluchter, Unbalanced Repeated-Measures Models with Structured Covariance Matrices|https://doi.org/10.2307/2530695]
